From a36982fd26ecf121e858d2ef4745f4f80249b36a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 16:40:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): honour the n_kv_max sparse hint in tiled
 flash attention prefill
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

With the n_kv_max hint, a long prefill now gathers only the KV cells that
its queries can see, as CUDA, Vulkan and Metal already do. Before, the
tiled path skipped a 64x64 tile only when all 64 rows masked it. Qwen QSA
rows keep different pools, so the work stayed dense, and the mask was
scanned and K/V repacked once per query head.

The new path runs in two phases.

Phase 1 builds one ascending list per group of 8 query tokens (the CUDA
ncols1) and per mask sequence: the cells that any of the 8 rows can see.
An AVX2 scan reads 16 F16 mask cells per load, with a scalar tail and a
scalar fallback. All KV heads share a list, so the mask is read once per
token group, not once per head.

Phase 2 hands out units of (token group, KV head, head block) with the
threadpool chunk counter. A head block holds at most 12 query heads, so
the 8 tokens and 12 heads of Qwen QSA form one 96-row Q block. The unit
gathers K/V for 64 listed cells at a time, once for all its heads, and
runs the tiled path's simd_gemm, online softmax, sinks and output code.
The work per unit grows with the union per group, not with n_kv. The
union is a subset of the dense cells, so the FLOPs never rise.

The gate is the CUDA one (F16 mask with ne[2] == 1, no ALiBi, no
softcap, n_kv >= 4096) on top of the tiled path's own (at least 64 query
rows, F32 Q, F16 or F32 K/V). This first version also needs head sizes
up to 256 and K/V with the same head counts. DeepSeek (512) and the MLA
shape (576/512) keep the tiled path. Decode and MTP verify (fewer than
64 rows) do not change.

The plan reserves the per-thread scratch and the lists through one size
helper in common.h, ggml_fa_sparse_wsize. The kernel takes the path only
if the work buffer holds that size. Each list holds min(n_kv, 8*n_kv_max)
cells, because the hint bounds each row by n_kv_max. A debug build
asserts that bound per row. In a release build, a list that overflows
sends the whole op back to the tiled path.

The output is numerically close to the tiled path, not bit-exact: the
online softmax rescales at different tile boundaries. KQ itself does not
change, because simd_gemm accumulates each element in the same order.
Ops outside the gate run the old code and stay bit-exact.

Tests: eval cases for a partial token group, sinks, two mask sequences,
F32 K/V, head blocks of 1, 8 and 7+6 query heads, large logits and a
pool-shaped QSA-like mask, plus two gate cases (ALiBi and softcap) that
must keep the tiled path. Perf cases for 256/256 gqa 12 at kv 4096,
32768 and 131072, with a random mask (worst-case union), a pool-shaped
mask and the dense tiled path to compare.

Credits: CUDA sparse flash attention (ggml-org #29298 and earlier),
Vulkan #28105, Metal #28098, and the gather idea of ik_llama.cpp #2404.

